      "writtenOn": "2026-10-27T13:53:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we can avoid using `SkMatrix` here. Using `this-\u003elocalToDeviceTransform()`, if it\u0027s type is `Type::kRectStaysRect` should mostly correspond to `isScaleTranslate()` (although it covers 90 degree rotations, but I think we want to snap that rect to pixels still).\n\nAnd for the else branch, `Transform` has already cached the minimum and maximum scale factors of the matrix, so we should use that instead of `decomposeScale` I think.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2564,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2564,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "9935d3408a4f55e259a7f95bb90d51bbd7d10803",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d6fd8ac6_edd401f7",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/Device.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 20
      },
      "lineNbr": 2564,
      "author": {
        "id": 3438316
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-27T17:36:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We do need to know which scale value is the x and which is y, and the minimum and maximum doesn\u0027t tell us that. It also doesn\u0027t seem like that data is stored anywhere in transform, and for decomposing the scale, I would have to add a function in `SkM44` or do that manually here which matches the `SkMatrix` behavior. I could go that route but it seems like it\u0027s simplest to keep it an `SkMatrix` otherwise and it is already cached in SkDevice.",
